MLLM description: The Mangrove Cuckoo is a medium-sized bird with a slender body, long tail, and a curved bill. Its plumage is predominantly brownish-grey on the upperparts, transitioning to a lighter, yellowish-brown on the underparts. The tail is long and black-and-white barred, which is a distinctive feature. The head has a dark eye-stripe and a pale supercilium. The legs are short and strong, adapted for perching in trees.

Behaviorally, the Mangrove Cuckoo is known for its distinctive call, often described as a "kuk-ku-kuk" or "kuk-ku-ku-ku-ku." It is typically found in mangrove forests, where it feeds on insects and small invertebrates. The Mangrove Cuckoo is also known for its brood parasitism, laying eggs in the nests of other birds, particularly those of the cuckoo family.

In summary, the Mangrove Cuckoo can be distinguished by its long, barred tail, brownish-grey and yellowish-brown plumage, and distinctive call. Its unique identifying trait is the black-and-white barred tail, which sets it apart from other cuckoo species.
