Behaviorally, Scott's Orioles are known to be territorial and often seen perched on branches or feeding on fruits and seeds. They are also known to build large, hanging nests made of plant fibers and spider webs.

In terms of size, they are slightly larger than a sparrow, with an average length of about 7 inches. Their proportions are compact, with a relatively short neck and a long tail.

The unique identifying traits include their bright coloration, especially the contrast between the yellow underparts and the olive-green upperparts, as well as the black cap and white supercilium. These features help distinguish them from other similar species like Bullock's Oriole, which has a more orange-yellow throat and breast, and Icterus parisorum, which has a more uniform olive-green coloration.
