MLLM description: The Swainson's Warbler is a small, slender bird with a compact body and short tail. Its plumage is predominantly olive-green above, transitioning to a lighter yellowish-green below. The head has a dark brown cap, and the face is marked with a thin white stripe extending from the eye to the back of the head. The wings are short and rounded, with a faint pinkish wash on the inner wing. The legs and feet are pale pink.

Behaviorally, Swainson's Warblers are active and agile, often seen foraging in dense vegetation or flying quickly through the underbrush. They are known for their distinctive song, which is a series of high-pitched, trilled notes.

Key identifying traits include its olive-green upperparts, pale yellowish-green underparts, and the dark brown cap on the head. The pinkish wash on the inner wing is also a distinguishing feature. The bird's size and proportions, being small and slender, further help in distinguishing it from other warbler species.
