The Worm-eating Warbler is often found in dense, low undergrowth in deciduous forests, where it forages for insects by probing into leaf litter and twigs with its bill. It is a secretive species, and its calls are often difficult to hear due to their soft, high-pitched trills. The bird's behavior is primarily focused on foraging and breeding, with little social interaction observed outside of these activities. Overall, the Worm-eating Warbler can be distinguished from other similar warblers by its unique head pattern and coloration, as well as its specific habitat and foraging habits.
